    "field": "Aerosol-climate interactions",
    "problem": "The potential contribution of anthropogenic iron oxide (FeOx) to shortwave atmospheric heating is unknown, as climate studies have largely considered only carbonaceous aerosols and natural mineral dust as the significant light-absorbing particles.",
    "method": "This study quantifies the abundance of anthropogenic iron oxide aerosols over East Asia using aircraft measurements with a modified single-particle soot photometer, and then calculates their shortwave absorbing power based on the observed size-resolved mass concentrations to evaluate their contribution to atmospheric heating.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the abundance and shortwave absorbing power of anthropogenic iron oxide aerosols over East Asia to determine their previously unknown contribution to atmospheric heating, particularly in relation to black carbon.",
